      "text": "1. We start by noting the properties of the imaginary unit \\(i\\):\n   \\[\n   i^1 = i, \\quad i^2 = -1, \\quad i^3 = -i, \\quad i^4 = 1\n   \\]\n   - These values repeat every \\(4\\) powers:\n   \\[\n   i^5 = i^1 = i, \\quad i^6 = i^2 = -1, \\quad i^7 = i^3 = -i, \\quad i^8 = i^4 = 1\n   \\]\n   \n2. We are given that \\(i^x + i^y\\) is a real number. The product of two imaginary units, \\(i^x\\) and \\(i^y\\), can be both positive or negative, but we are only interested in sums that are real.\n\n3. To find the combinations where \\(i^x + i^y\\) is real, we need to consider the pairs \\((i^x, i^y)\\) with corresponding exponents. Since \\(i^x\\) and \\(i^y\\) must add up to a real number, the powers of \\(i\\) must have the same sine and cosine functions because:\n   \\[\n   \\cos \\theta + i \\sin \\theta \\quad \\text{is real}\n   \\]\n\n4. Therefore, the combined sum \\(\\cos \\theta_1 + i \\sin \\theta_1 + \\cos \\theta_2 + i \\sin \\theta_2\\) must result in a real number. Hence:\n   \\[\n   \\cos(\\theta_1 + \\theta_2) + i \\sin(\\theta_1 + \\theta_2)\n   \\]\n   \\(\\cos \\theta_1 + \\cos \\theta_2\\) must be real only if \\(\\theta_1\\) and \\(-\\theta_1\\) have the same value (e.g., \\(\\theta_1 = \\theta_2 = 0\\) implying \\(\\cos 0 + \\cos 0 = 2\\) which is real).\n\n5. We identify pairs \\((i^x, i^y)\\) where the exponents \\(\\theta\\):\n   \\[\n   \\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\frac{5\\pi}{2} \\ldots\n   \\]\n   \\(\\theta_1 = \\theta_2\\) where:\n   \\[\n   \\theta_1 = 0, \\quad \\theta_2 = 2\\pi, \\quad \\theta_3 = -2\\pi\n   \\]\n   etc., hence \\(\\theta_1 = \\theta_2 = 0\\) or \\(\\theta_1\\). The pairs add to either 0 or \\(\\pi\\).\n\n6. Combining these with our increasing ranges given by:\n   \\[\n   1 \\le x < y \\le 100\n   \\]\n   This condition can form the pairs \\((0, 2), (2, 0), (1, 2), (2, 1)\\).\n\n7. Thus, one can now count how many ordered pairs exist:\n   \\[\n   (x, y) = (1, 2)\n   \\]\n   There are:\n   \\[\n   1 \\text{ ordered pair}\n   \\]\n   Permutations can be calculated as:\n   \\[\n   1 \\times 2 = 2\n   \\]\n\nConclusion: Therefore, the number of ordered pairs is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]",
